A||N||22||Voytek||Most Common A/C Problems On Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||Voytek||07-10-2001||02:18 PM||||Hey Everyone<BR>I'm just trying to fix and convert my A/C.  Believe it or not, it does get pretty warm around here.<BR>I believe I need a new compressor, maybe the pressure switch too.  Other than that an accumulator and all new lines/fittings for the conversion.  Since the A/C is dead right now, I am not 100% sure what the problem is.  I hate going to shops for A/C problems as my parents have been ripped off twice before, $500 bucks each time and the system never worked properly.<BR>Has anyone had problems with their A/C before?  Does anyone know what the most common problems with Fiero A/C are?  Are there any silly little things to watch out for?  How hard is it to change the parts over to the R134 compatible?<BR>Thanks for any info.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||07-10-2001||02:32 PM||||I am doing the conversion right now however I am waiting for my buddies air compressor so I can blow-out all the lines.  It is pretty straight forward.  One thing you might want to do is to check to see if your compressor works.  You can find the A/C conversion outlined in Ogre's cave.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||07-10-2001||03:07 PM||||I just got my 88GT back from the GM service center. They checked for leaks and added 2.5 pounds of R12. They also added a die into the mix to find the leak. After all was said and done. I gave them $200.00. ($50.00 a pound for R12.)  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Leadfoot||07-10-2001||03:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WisconsinGT:<BR><B>I gave them $200.00. ($50.00 a pound for R12.)  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ouch!  How would that compare to R134.  I don't know what the going rate is but I would think it would be much cheaper.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-10-2001||04:38 PM||||R-134 cost about $4.50 per pound.  The conversion is pretty simple but it does require you to evacuate the old freon out of the system.  Legally this requires recycling the old freon.  If yours is gone already then it is a moot point.<P>If you have an air compressor you can get an air eductor vacuum pump from Harbour Freight for around $10. US to do the job.  You'll need an R-12 to R-134 adapter fitting to connect the R-134 hose connection to the vacuum eductor.  I got one at a local A/C repair shop for $4.00 US.<P>Does your system have any pressure on it at all?  You can check by removing the cap from the low pressure side shraeder fitting (looks like a tire stem valve) located on the dryer canister (silver colored aluminum can under the front hood on the firewall near the windshield washer bottle) and pressing the center pin.  <P>Forgive me if this is way too basic for you but I don't know what your mechanical skills are.<P>If there is pressure then you can either have some expensive R-12 installed to see if it will hold pressure and if the compressor will start or have it vacuumed down and do the R-134 conversion yourself.  The conversion kits run around $35 US and can be gotten at WalMart/K-Mart or most any auto parts stores.<P><BR>Sorry, got to get back to work for now.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Leadfoot||07-10-2001||04:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B>Does your system have any pressure on it at all? ..... <P>Forgive me if this is way too basic for you but I don't know what your mechanical skills are.<P>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 07-10-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My system had no pressure and there was no oil in the accumulator.  The compressor was completely shot, which explains why the filter was plugged with metal shavings.<P>Don't worry if your instructions are too basic.  Some people are looking for such information.  As for me, well I have been known to turn a mean left-hand phillips screw driver and I know how to replace those dreaded muffler bearings everybody is talking about.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||07-10-2001||04:55 PM||||IF YOU PRESS THE VALVE, MAKE SURE YOUR EYES ARE PROTECTED AND USE A SCREWDRIVER OR SOMETHING SO IT DONT HIT YOUR FINGER. FREON CAN FREEZE TISSUE IN AN INSTANT.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||07-10-2001||05:26 PM||||Too late.<P>Just kidding, but I did use my finger and there was stuff in there but didn't freeze anything off.<P>Wow, some good replies.  I would still like to know if Fieros had any specific A/C problems.  Also, if you short the pressure switch, someone here said that the compressor should kick in.  Well, I've done that and it did not kick in.  Does that mean a bad switch or a bad compressor?<P>I also went and visited Ogre's conversion info.  Quite thorough but doesn't cover everything.  He says that the hoses do not necessarily need to be replaced because, once they're saturated with the old oil, they should not bypass the R134.  Does that mean 'definitely no, don't need to replace them' or 'should, but don't have to'?  Does the Napa kit come with the hoses?  Does the kit come with instructions on oiling the compressor (I'm not sure what is meant by that but Ogre does talk about that)?<P>Also, any tips on taking out the compressor?  I've heard the thing is VERY heavy.  I'm sure I can figure it out, but I would like to be as prepared as I can.<P>I want this thing to be done as right as possible and be as freezing as it can be, but don't want to pay someone the money to do it.<P>Thanks everyone.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||07-10-2001||10:28 PM||||I'm in the same boat here. Reading everything I can. Still not decided if doing it myself (convert) or paying someone. How much does the R134 compatible acc. cost? That needs to be added to the kit cost.<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||skitime||07-11-2001||06:02 AM||||I just had replaced my seized compressor ced and had a place convert it to R134. It worked great for a short time.  Yesterday I took it back and the seals on the hose end of the compressor were leaking. They replaced the seals and it is working fine again. My advise is replace all the seals (four O rings) before charging the system and put in dye so that if there are leaks they can easily be found. Remember these seals are getting old.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||07-11-2001||12:23 PM||||The hoses do not have to be replaced unless they are leaking.<P>I don't know of any peculararities of the Fiero A/C system.  They aren't more prone to failure than any other GM unit.  The problem is that the compressor is rather a pain to get to compared to front engined GM cars.<P>I believe the compressor weighs around 30 pounds.  Its not real heave but it can seem so when you are holding it up from under the car.<P>The compressor on my 85SE v-6 4spd locked up about the same time I decided to refresh the motor and replace the clutch.  So while I had the cradle dropped I flushed all the hoses using the proper flush and an air hose.  I installed a new drier and I replaced all the o-rings and the expansion valve. The o-rings are not difficult to get to in the engine compartment.  The ones in the front compartment required me to remove the spare tire bay liner.  If you do this be sure to disconnect the brake line from the clips attached to the underside of the liner.<P>If you install a new compressor you might as well replace the oil with the proper R-134 oil if the compressor does not already have it.  Ask the supplier what oil it will comes with.<P>Most of the kits on the market today include a can of R-134 oil charge which gets installed like the freon.  If you are putting in a new compressor simply pour out as much of the old oil as you can from the hose ports.  Then pour in an 8 oz. can of oil (you'll have to buy the oil in a can or bottle, not in the freon type can) and turn the compressor shaft by hand about 10 turns to get it distributed in the housing.<P>Flush the lines and install a new expansion valve and drier.  Vacuum it down to about 28.5 inches and charge with R-134.<P>I had to tighten the top bolts on the compressor on my 85GT.  My battery tray was rotted away and there was no battery insulator.  This gave me some room to get my hands on it from the top.  You might try removing the battery tray insulator (the plastic box looking piece that shields the battery from the exhaust manifold) to get better access.<P>You asked about directions supplied with the kit.  The instructions will be specific to the kit.  When I replaced the compressor on my SE I bought a kit that included o-rings, service port adapters, a bottle of oil, and instructions based on installing a new compressor.  Most of the kits you see in WalMart, etc. are for simply replacing the existing freon in an R-12 system without opening the system.  So you might want to check with a Parts Store instead of a place like WalMart.<P>I've done 4 of my cars using various kits and they all work fine.  The work is not hard to do.  Go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek||07-11-2001||03:48 PM||||Thank you guys.  Those were some helpful replies - especially that last one.  We'll see how things go.<BR>||||reg||1
