Z||000014||bHooper||07-11-2001||07:24 PM||||the a/c on my "new" '86 SE was converted over to 134, about 6-12 months b4 i bought it.  this car barely cools good enough for north carolina.  it doesn't "feel" like it blows all that hard either.<P>is this a sign of the compressor and blower motor going?<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000015||cowans||07-12-2001||12:13 PM||||I just converted to 134a and there is an important difference with a "D6" compressor.<BR>My shop replaced the dead D6 compressor, Drier and filter screen & O rings. Then they flushed the entire system, evacuated and charged the system. The Fiero, because of the piping (front to back) requires a large amount to charge (3.4 lbs) I believe. Nice cold air for 1.5 kms and bang!!! blew the rubber joint by the compressor... replaced the offending hose.... evacuated and recharged.... nice cold air for about 30 kms, and BANG!!! compressor seized up and V8 (on the highway) ate up the compressor clutch. New compressor replaced (shop is having major ****fits on what to do!! Well, Guess what!! For the D6 compressor, it is recommended to only recharge 90% when using 134a.... so they pulled back 6 oz of 134a and system runs fine!! Hard lesson for shop($$$) but they warrantied their mistake! Apparently, when using a full charge, some of the gas can convert back to a liquid BEFORE it hits the compressor, thereby locking up your "NEW" compressor!<BR>Sandy||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||07-12-2001||02:25 PM||||All of the R-134 conversion kits I've used recommend using about 85% to 90% of the normal R-12 charge volume.  Too much freon will raise the high pressure side too high for the system to handle and something will let go.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Voytek||07-13-2001||10:27 AM||||How do I check that the A/C place charged the system with the proper amount?  I mean, I'm doing all the work myself (replacing the compressor, drier, fittings).  I will need to ensure that they charge it properly, otherwise I'm stuck with a blown system, or worse, a blown compressor.<BR>Is there a recommended pressure (system running / not running) that can be checked at the bleed valve?<BR>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Voytek||07-13-2001||10:31 AM||||And one more question.  Cowans - you say the system was flushed after replacing the compressor.<BR>What I don't understand is this: the purpose of the flush is to remove all of the old oil, right?  The new compressor will be filled with the new oil.  Would this flush not remove the new oil from the compressor?<BR>Is it not a good idea to get the system flushed BEFORE replacing any components?<BR>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Alex4mula||07-13-2001||11:38 AM||||Which car uses the D6 compressor? How much does the dryer cost?? TIA.<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||cowans||07-13-2001||11:42 AM||||Voytek, perhaps what I previously stated was in the wrong order but in any case to answer your question on oil,.... once your system is tight, you evacuate your system with a AC vacumn,(I think 29 inches).(the more evacuation time the better(drier system). The oil can then be added through the evacuator machine (because you have vacumn) then recharged. I believe the Fiero holds about 3.4lbs R12 so with 134a use 10% less, about -6oz. Run the car/air on for 10-15 minutes and watch the Hi/Lo gauges on evacutor machine(can't remember the #'s, someone help me out, maybe settles out at  70???). Hope I have been some help!!<BR>Sandy||||reg||1
Z||000021||Voytek||07-13-2001||11:55 AM||||First - I appreciate all the info.<P>Ok.  So let me relate this to my scenario.  My system is dead but still has some Freon in it.  So I would go to a place to recycle the Freon.  Then, I would change all the necessary components.  Then, I would go to a shop (I can't do this myself) and get the system vacuumed, have them add the oil to the system and recharge at the same time?  What about the oil that comes with the conversion kit?  Someone here said that you have to put some of that in the new compressor.  Is that true?  There are a couple of reasons why I want to be absolutely sure that this is done right the first time.<BR>#1: I don't want the system to break down any time soon.<BR>#2: I have seen a few REALLY crooked shops when it comes to A/C.  They may (for example) not charge the system properly and tell me that I need another part which they can install for a great price.  OR tell me that the system does not work because my order was incorrect when, in reality, they screwed something up.<BR>Etc, etc.  I've heard a few of these before.<BR>Anyways, THE MORE INPUT THE BETTER - THANKS!||||reg||1
Z||000022||cowans||07-13-2001||01:43 PM||||Voytek...about the conversion kit oil. As you  replace the O-rings the conversion kit states to add 1 oz to the (ex: condenser, compressor, etc.).... as you proceed. make note of exactly how much you have added. The shop will adjust what you have added to a total of (6 oz), I believe! I will state that I am in no way a AC mechanic, I just have had a lesson/lately. When the shop evacuates the system, the vacumn is only removing any moisture that has accumulated in the system....not any oil!!! Some words of wisdom.... have the shop install your new drier when they are ready to proceed with the recharge... the drier is basically a canister with dessicant in it, no need to let the dessicant absorb more moisture by installing it a couple of days before recharge. You can also purchase/install a stop-leak dye, they are pretty expensive kits though. There is a $6 filter screen in the pipe fitting by the evaporator( where your heater core is ), remove it carefully and check the screen for the remains of a dead compressor (small pieces of metal). It basically transforms the liquid freon back to a gas. Good luck!!!<BR>Sandy<BR>||||reg||1
